[Both laughing]

00:01:44.551 --> 00:01:47.637
This was my first year as an NBA

00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:50.807
voter. For my vote for Defensive Player of the Year—

00:01:51.558 --> 00:01:52.642
you know you're my guy.

00:01:53.059 --> 00:01:55.228
I voted Rudy [Gobert] one,

00:01:56.604 --> 00:01:59.607
Ben Simmons two and

00:01:59.607 --> 00:02:01.192
Bam Adebayo three.

00:02:02.735 --> 00:02:05.572
Why was I wrong in not including

00:02:05.572 --> 00:02:06.614
you?

00:02:06.614 --> 00:02:09.200
- Oh, well, clearly you're analyzing the game

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:10.201
like some of these people.

00:02:10.201 --> 00:02:13.079
- [Laughing]

00:02:13.913 --> 00:02:16.624
- Why were you wrong? Because you didn't name, not one person,

00:02:16.624 --> 00:02:18.751
that can control the game from the defensive

00:02:18.751 --> 00:02:19.627
side of the floor.

00:02:19.627 --> 00:02:21.588
- You don't think that Rudy Gobert does that?

00:02:21.838 --> 00:02:24.632
- I think what he does on the defensive side of the ball

00:02:24.757 --> 00:02:27.760
is incredible. I think the way he protects

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.179
the rim is incredible.

00:02:29.679 --> 00:02:32.015
Defense is more than just protecting the rim.

00:02:32.182 --> 00:02:34.309
Y'all don't judge defense the same way y'all judge offense.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.895
There is no offensive situation
- That's true.
- where you don't want Steph

00:02:37.353 --> 00:02:40.356
Curry on the floor. There's no offensive situation where

00:02:40.356 --> 00:02:41.774
you don't want James Harden on the floor.

00:02:42.066 --> 00:02:44.944
There's no offensive situation where you don't want

00:02:45.486 --> 00:02:47.322
LeBron James on the floor.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.200
Well, guess what? There's no defensive situation where you

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.201
don't want me on the floor.

00:02:51.576 --> 00:02:52.827
Everybody, look. Oh, man.

00:02:52.869 --> 00:02:55.705
This guy led the league in steals.

00:02:55.747 --> 00:02:59.334
That guy led the league in blocks.
But is there a situation you can put that guy

00:02:59.334 --> 00:03:01.085
in and make him uncomfortable? For most guys, yes.

00:03:01.085 --> 00:03:03.296
You can't put me in a situation on defense and make me

00:03:03.296 --> 00:03:04.297
uncomfortable.

00:03:05.131 --> 00:03:08.134
- So we played the normal "Heads Up!" But we have scoured

00:03:08.134 --> 00:03:10.470
the internet. We've scoured your Instagram, your Twitter,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.890
all these things and made some Draymond custom packs.

00:03:14.432 --> 00:03:15.016
- OK.

00:03:15.016 --> 00:03:16.309
- So are you ready for those?

00:03:16.601 --> 00:03:17.852
- I gotta know my own life.

00:03:18.102 --> 00:03:18.561
All right.

00:03:18.561 --> 00:03:19.979
- I'll give clues first.

00:03:20.396 --> 00:03:21.397
Close your eyes.

00:03:22.148 --> 00:03:23.566
Oh, the Pittsburgh football team.

00:03:24.692 --> 00:03:25.568
- Steelers.

00:03:25.568 --> 00:03:26.569
- Yep.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.824
The "blank" brothers. They're your teammates,

00:03:33.993 --> 00:03:36.246
- The "blank" brothers...the Splash Brothers!

00:03:36.329 --> 00:03:37.330
- Yes, yes. Um, this

00:03:39.499 --> 00:03:42.460
is like when you do something funny and

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.629
we make it a picture on the internet, it's called a—?

00:03:44.754 --> 00:03:45.463
- A meme.

00:03:45.463 --> 00:03:46.464
- Yes.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.179
This is what you have. You put 'em on your finger.

00:03:53.554 --> 00:03:55.014
You champ. You have this on your fingers.

00:03:55.014 --> 00:03:55.932
- Rings.

00:03:55.932 --> 00:03:56.849
- Yes.

00:03:56.849 --> 00:03:57.225
- Big rings.

00:03:57.225 --> 00:03:58.685
- Big rings.

00:03:59.018 --> 00:04:02.021
OK, you get these when you want your toes to look good.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:03.731
- Pedicure.

00:04:03.731 --> 00:04:04.732
- Yep.

00:04:04.732 --> 00:04:05.733
- I need one bad.

00:04:06.985 --> 00:04:09.737
You are on this. You were just on it last night after the

00:04:09.779 --> 00:04:10.697
game.

00:04:10.947 --> 00:04:12.198
- TNT.

00:04:12.198 --> 00:04:12.865
- Yes. NBA on TNT. Yes.

00:04:12.865 --> 00:04:14.033
- Inside the NBA.

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:15.285
- Yep. Oh.

00:04:15.326 --> 00:04:17.870
When you win, the whole city comes out for the—?

00:04:18.162 --> 00:04:19.163
- Parade.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:22.458
- Yeah! OK, we did, we did well. We did well.

00:04:22.583 --> 00:04:25.628
OK, if you had to tell me your top five players

00:04:25.628 --> 00:04:28.006
under 25, who would you say?

00:04:29.048 --> 00:04:30.883
- Luka. Jayson Tatum for sure.

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:31.968
Zion.

00:04:31.968 --> 00:04:34.429
- Of course Devin Booker. Can't forget Devin Booker.

00:04:34.971 --> 00:04:35.513
- Book, for sure.

00:04:35.513 --> 00:04:36.389
- Yeah.

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:38.891
Luka, Jayson Tatum, Zion, Book.

00:04:39.559 --> 00:04:42.562
And you gotta be hatin' if

00:04:42.562 --> 00:04:45.106
you, if you can't put Trae Young in that category right now.

00:04:45.356 --> 00:04:48.109
- He's taken on this kind of villain persona, as everybody

00:04:48.109 --> 00:04:50.945
continues to say. I think that for some people,

00:04:50.945 --> 00:04:52.989
you are the villain, right?

00:04:53.114 --> 00:04:55.116
For them and their cities, for their teams.

00:04:55.116 --> 00:04:57.785
What has it been like for you to see Trae kind of

00:04:57.785 --> 00:04:59.996
encapsulate that personality?

00:04:59.996 --> 00:05:03.207
And what advice would you have for him

00:05:03.207 --> 00:05:04.459
about playing that role?

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:07.920
- Not only has he taken on that role, but

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.465
he's producing. You know, he walking off the court:

00:05:10.465 --> 00:05:11.966
I see y'all in the A.

00:05:11.966 --> 00:05:14.427
Like, he talking big boy stuff.

00:05:14.677 --> 00:05:17.722
I love that. You know. To see him talking

00:05:17.847 --> 00:05:19.390
it, like, and he walking it.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:22.685
As far as him taking on that villain role, like,

00:05:22.685 --> 00:05:24.687
enjoy it. It's a beautiful thing.

00:05:24.687 --> 00:05:27.106
But know it, you know, it comes with a lot of shit.

00:05:27.106 --> 00:05:29.150
- Tell me what it comes with.

00:05:29.525 --> 00:05:32.111
- A lot of hate. A lot of responsibility.

00:05:32.236 --> 00:05:35.198
Because, I mean, shame on the man that go

00:05:35.198 --> 00:05:36.616
talking and can't walk it.

00:05:36.616 --> 00:05:39.077
When you have a bad game and you're not the villain?

00:05:39.077 --> 00:05:41.746
Ahh, he had a bad game. All right,
whatever. Move to the next.

00:05:41.871 --> 00:05:44.707
But when you have a bad game
and you the villain and

00:05:44.707 --> 00:05:45.917
everybody wanna shit on you?

00:05:46.084 --> 00:05:48.795
But you gotta block that noise out.

00:05:48.795 --> 00:05:51.714
But at the same time as you blocking it out,
you're also reading it

00:05:51.714 --> 00:05:53.716
because you need it as fuel, like, you need

00:05:53.716 --> 00:05:54.717
to prove them wrong.

00:05:54.717 --> 00:05:58.513
- What percentage of guys in the league that are the villains

00:05:58.513 --> 00:06:00.765
do you think decided to be the villain?

00:06:00.765 --> 00:06:03.518
And what percentage of those guys were forced to

00:06:03.518 --> 00:06:04.811
be the villain?

00:06:04.811 --> 00:06:07.313
- Like Patrick Beverley decided to be a villain.

00:06:07.313 --> 00:06:10.191
- Like, in fairness to him,

00:06:10.191 --> 00:06:12.819
he probably wouldn't be in the NBA if he didn't

00:06:12.819 --> 00:06:16.197
decide that. Not because he's not a good player,

00:06:16.489 --> 00:06:18.408
but that's how he made his name.

00:06:18.658 --> 00:06:21.285
A guy like Trae has decided to be the villain,

00:06:21.411 --> 00:06:24.038
which is cool cuz you taking on that.
- Yeah.
- And that may

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:27.375
motivate you, you know. And there are times

00:06:27.417 --> 00:06:29.377
in your career where that changes as well.

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.963
You know, like when KD came to the Warriors,

00:06:32.296 --> 00:06:34.382
he got thrown into being a villain

00:06:34.715 --> 00:06:38.052
because everybody's mad that he came to the Warriors.

00:06:38.177 --> 00:06:41.389
So all of a sudden, everybody hates him. Now he's this.

00:06:41.639 --> 00:06:44.475
He's that. He's a coward. All the blah, blah, blah,

00:06:44.475 --> 00:06:47.353
blah, blah. You're thrown into that because

00:06:47.353 --> 00:06:49.063
of a decision you decide to make with your life.

00:06:50.648 --> 00:06:53.317
- All right. Close your eyes, Draymond. This is like an app

00:06:53.317 --> 00:06:55.361
where you can take a pic, but it goes away.

00:06:55.945 --> 00:06:56.821
- Snapchat.

00:06:56.821 --> 00:06:57.738
- Yep.

00:06:57.738 --> 00:07:01.492
- Y'all are foul.
[Both laughing]

00:07:02.452 --> 00:07:03.619
Oh, that's crazy.

00:07:03.619 --> 00:07:05.872
They said I gave Steven Adams a what?

00:07:07.707 --> 00:07:08.791
Like if you punch

00:07:08.791 --> 00:07:10.585
somebody below the belt in boxing.

00:07:11.461 --> 00:07:13.212
- Like a groin hit?

00:07:13.296 --> 00:07:14.922
- If it's not high, it's what?

00:07:14.922 --> 00:07:17.008
- Low. Oh, low blow.

00:07:17.592 --> 00:07:19.510
- I like to ride the bike.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:21.512
It is a part of Equinox.

00:07:21.512 --> 00:07:23.473
- Oh, um, Peloton.

00:07:23.473 --> 00:07:26.100
- What's the proper word for a bike?

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.685
- Uh, cycle?

00:07:27.685 --> 00:07:29.520
- SoulCycle!

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.063
- You SoulCycle, Draymond?

00:07:31.063 --> 00:07:33.691
- I used to SoulCycle all the time. I Peloton a lot now,

00:07:33.691 --> 00:07:34.484
though.

00:07:35.359 --> 00:07:38.696
- You know, we have to talk about this recent fan

00:07:38.696 --> 00:07:41.157
aggression at games.

00:07:41.491 --> 00:07:44.785
I know it's easy to say, you know, what you would do if you

00:07:44.785 --> 00:07:47.872
were in these situations when you aren't actually in the

00:07:47.872 --> 00:07:49.332
situation specifically.

00:07:49.332 --> 00:07:51.667
But do you think it would ever get to the point for you

00:07:51.667 --> 00:07:54.587
where you would confront a fan?

00:07:54.587 --> 00:07:57.673
- I'm already judged a little harder in this league on

00:07:57.673 --> 00:08:01.135
things, so I try to stay as far away as I can.

00:08:01.135 --> 00:08:04.180
But, you know, when you got somebody throwing stuff on you,

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:07.183
throwing stuff right in your face, like, how can

00:08:07.183 --> 00:08:08.726
you not say somethin'?

00:08:08.726 --> 00:08:11.103
You know we've had these complaints to the league for years,

00:08:11.103 --> 00:08:13.773
to the league for years, because no one has seen it.

00:08:13.773 --> 00:08:15.566
Nothing's been done,

00:08:15.566 --> 00:08:19.237
which is why it's escalated to where it is today,

00:08:19.237 --> 00:08:22.949
because everybody's been protected, except the players.

00:08:22.949 --> 00:08:25.243
You know, somebody can leave their house and say, “I'mma

00:08:25.284 --> 00:08:28.704
go talk shit to Draymond and say everything

00:08:28.746 --> 00:08:31.332
I wanna say to him, because if he say something back,

00:08:31.332 --> 00:08:32.708
he loses money.”

00:08:32.708 --> 00:08:35.920
So, you know, they say, “Oh, such and such guy, the fan

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.423
got arrested.” Well, that simply means he was handcuffed

00:08:39.423 --> 00:08:40.758
and taken out the building.

00:08:40.758 --> 00:08:44.470
Everybody and their mom think they can play basketball

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:46.973
or think they know basketball.
- Mmhmm.

00:08:46.973 --> 00:08:49.433
- And I'm just always like, if these people came and sat at

00:08:49.433 --> 00:08:52.562
the table with me and talked basketball, they’d think

00:08:52.562 --> 00:08:55.147
they was talking to Elon Musk about space.

00:08:55.147 --> 00:08:56.148
- [Laughing] Yes.

00:08:56.148 --> 00:09:00.820
- Like, like, I study this every day of my life.

00:09:00.987 --> 00:09:02.655
I study basketball.

00:09:02.655 --> 00:09:05.825
You can't possibly think you can just come to a game one

00:09:05.825 --> 00:09:08.995
day a week or twice a week and know what I know about this.

00:09:10.204 --> 00:09:13.416
- Answer these questions as quickly as you can.

00:09:13.416 --> 00:09:14.333
You ready?

00:09:14.333 --> 00:09:15.459
- All right. Let's do it.

00:09:15.459 --> 00:09:15.960
- OK.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:17.295
Fill in the blank.

00:09:17.295 --> 00:09:19.338
Draymond Green is—

00:09:19.839 --> 00:09:21.757
- A champion.
- Mmm.

00:09:21.757 --> 00:09:23.175
First pair of sneakers?

00:09:23.175 --> 00:09:24.844
- The first pair of sneakers I remember,

00:09:24.844 --> 00:09:26.929
I think I was in the first grade when I really cared.

00:09:26.929 --> 00:09:28.806
The Gloves came out.

00:09:28.806 --> 00:09:31.726
I couldn't get ‘em, so I had, like, these—like, my

00:09:31.726 --> 00:09:34.520
brother got ‘em. I got in trouble at school, so I had these

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:37.315
terrible, like, Nike knockoff shoes.

00:09:37.315 --> 00:09:39.400
And I remember that pair of sneakers because it was just

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.153
the most traumatizing pair of sneakers in my life.

00:09:42.153 --> 00:09:45.615
- [Laughing] OK. Childhood crush?

00:09:45.615 --> 00:09:47.533
- Halle Berry.

00:09:47.533 --> 00:09:48.743
- Great choice.

00:09:48.743 --> 00:09:50.620
- Oh, and Chilli from TLC.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:51.829
I loved Chilli.

00:09:51.829 --> 00:09:52.872
- Yes, love Chilli.

00:09:52.872 --> 00:09:54.123
- She was incredible to me.

00:09:54.123 --> 00:09:55.833
- Biggest career W?

00:09:55.833 --> 00:09:58.628
- Game 6, 2015, at Cleveland.

00:09:58.628 --> 00:10:00.630
- Your biggest L?

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:03.007
- The 2016 NBA Finals.

00:10:03.007 --> 00:10:05.134
- Biggest inspiration?

00:10:05.134 --> 00:10:07.553
- My biggest inspiration now is my children.

00:10:07.553 --> 00:10:10.514
- Advice to your rookie self?

00:10:10.514 --> 00:10:13.517
- Do it exactly the way you did it.

00:10:13.517 --> 00:10:16.729
- I love that. Do it exactly the way you did it. Do not

00:10:16.729 --> 00:10:18.189
change a thing.

00:10:18.189 --> 00:10:20.066
- Absolutely not.

00:10:20.066 --> 00:10:22.818
- Well, perfect. Well, thank you so much for coming

00:10:22.818 --> 00:10:25.863
on “Just Playin’,” Draymond Green, Defensive Player of

00:10:25.863 --> 00:10:27.573
the Year. I appreciate it.

00:10:27.573 --> 00:10:28.949
- Thank you. I appreciate it. Thanks for having me.

00:10:28.949 --> 00:10:29.825
- [Laughing]

00:10:29.825 --> 00:10:31.285
- Get your vote right next year.

00:10:31.285 --> 00:10:33.496
I will make sure you do.

